WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
A few genes have functional copies in humans, and not in chimpanzees.

2
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.660
Do any of them change what progenitors do?

3
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:15.073
No gene has been removed from a developing human brain.

4
00:00:15.913 --> 00:00:17.833
So each was tested in another system.

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:21.233
First, ARHGAP11B.

6
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.953
In embryonic mouse cortex, it increased basal progenitors.

7
00:00:26.853 --> 00:00:29.613
In marmoset fetuses, it enlarged the neocortex.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:35.357
In cultured human fetal slices, blocking it reduced basal progenitors.

9
00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:39.547
Next, NOTCH2NL, which raises Notch signalling.

10
00:00:40.547 --> 00:00:44.347
In mouse cortex, it kept progenitors dividing and delayed neurons.

11
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:49.597
Deleted in human organoids, it made progenitors differentiate early.

12
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.810
Both are consistent with more doubling.

13
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.190
But the studies described here did not score division modes, so none shows which

14
00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.270
kind of division changed.

15
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:05.843
Time matters too. Neurogenesis takes about a week in the mouse, roughly two months

16
00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:08.143
in the rhesus monkey, and longer in humans.

17
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:13.233
Extra time allows more rounds, if cell cycles do not lengthen.

18
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:15.793
A steady pool adds in step with time.

19
00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:17.873
A growing pool compounds.

20
00:01:18.967 --> 00:01:21.447
One more difference lies outside the rule.

21
00:01:22.407 --> 00:01:23.807
Human neurons mature slowly.

22
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:29.607
Spine density in prefrontal cortex stays above adult levels into the third decade.

23
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.630
SRGAP2C slowed spine maturation in mouse neurons.

24
00:01:35.683 --> 00:01:38.023
Is that pace set by the body, or the cells?

25
00:01:40.443 --> 00:01:42.743
Human neurons go into newborn mouse cortex.

26
00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:44.703
Whose pace do they keep?

27
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:54.297
They matured over many months, close to a human time course.

28
00:01:55.157 --> 00:01:57.837
The mouse neurons around them had long since matured.

29
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:01.427
At least part of the timing travels with the cells.

30
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.070
But maturation shapes connections, not neuron number.

31
00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:11.070
It lies outside the rule.

32
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.350
And no test shows that one gene made the human cortex.

33
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:20.473
Multiply, don't add: a leading explanation for our large cortex.

34
00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:26.053
How much came from founders, outer radial glia, or a longer clock is still open.
